with B1 without B2 and B3, but B3 is already used.\n\nSo B1 is dead tile.\n\nWhereas T5, T6, T8 can form T5-T6-T7 or T6-T7-T8 or T7-T8-T9 (though T7 scarce).\n\nW3,W4 can form W3-W4-W5.\n\nHonors: F3 is golden (prevalent wind), keep for potential pung or pair.\n\nF2 and J1 are risky but may become pairs.\n\nBut B1 is completely stranded.\n\nMoreover, Pattern \"Outside Hand\" requires terminals or honors in every group \u2014 you have a middle-number chow (B3-B4-B5), so not outside.\n\nSo no benefit.\n\nThus, discard B1.\n\nValid action: Play B1\n\nBut wait \u2014 is \"Play B1\" in valid actions? Yes.\n\nAlso, you already played B1 in history \u2014 yes, \"Play B1\" \u2014 so you discarded B1 before?\n\nYour history: Play F3, Chi B4, Play B1, Play T2, Play F1\n\nYes, you played B1 earlier.\n\nBut now you have B1:1 in hand?\n\nImpossible unless you drew another.\n\nBut B1: total 4\n\nUnshown:2 \u2192 two left\n\nYou played one \u2192 one gone\n\nIf you now hold one, then two accounted, two unshown \u2192 but unshown=2 \u2192 ok.\n\nYes: total 4\n- 1 discarded by you (history)\n- 1 in your hand\n- 2 unshown (in wall or others)\n\nPerfectly consistent.\n\nSo you discarded one B1, then later drew another \u2014 rare, but possible.\n\nSo you can discard it again.\n\nTherefore, B1 is safe to discard.\n\nSimilarly, T2: you played T2, unshown T2:3 \u2192 one discarded, you don't hold T2 now? Hand has no T2 \u2014 ok.\n\nSo no issue.\n\nFinal decision:\n\n**Discard B1** \u2014 it's a stranded terminal tile with no developmental potential in your hand.\n\nEvaluation: B1 is a terminal tile that cannot form a chow with existing tiles due to gaps, and keeping it hinders the development of more flexible sequences. Other tiles like W3, W4, T5, T6 offer potential for forming chows in their respective suits. Honors like F3 (prevalent wind) should be preserved for scoring opportunities.\n\nReason: B1 is dead weight; it cannot contribute to any chow or pung in the current hand configuration, while other discards would break promising sequences.\n\nAnswer: Play B1"}
